[THOUGHT]  
The dialogue presents a focused clinical evaluation of a 3-month history of right thumb/wrist pain radiating to the elbow, exacerbated by infant holding (gripping + lifting), with no sensory symptoms (no numbness/tingling), no signs of inflammation (no swelling, warmth, redness), and no stiffness. The **Finkelstein’s test is positive**, which is a highly specific physical exam finding for **de Quervain tenosynovitis** — an overuse inflammation of the abductor pollicis longus (APL) and extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) tendons in the first dorsal compartment of the wrist. Absence of systemic or neurological features (e.g., no median nerve symptoms, normal Tinel/Phalen not assessed but NCS/EMG ordered), lack of inflammatory signs, and activity-specific aggravation strongly support a mechanical/tendinous etiology rather than carpal tunnel syndrome, arthritis, gout, infection, or neuropathy.

No imaging or lab results are reported — only tests *ordered*. Therefore, the diagnosis remains clinically suspected pending confirmatory studies (ultrasound/MRI being most relevant for de Quervain), but the clinical picture is classic.

[SUMMARY]  
**Clinical Impression:**  
- **Highly probable de Quervain tenosynovitis** of the right first dorsal compartment (involving APL and EPB tendons), based on:  
  • 3-month history of radial-sided wrist/thumb pain radiating proximally;  
  • Pain exacerbated by gripping and lifting (especially infant holding);  
  • Negative neurologic symptoms (no numbness/tingling in median nerve distribution);  
  • Absence of local inflammatory signs (no swelling, warmth, erythema);  
  • No morning stiffness or systemic features;  
  • **Positive Finkelstein’s test** — gold-standard provocative maneuver with high specificity (>90%).  

**Pending Investigations (ordered but results not yet available):**  
- Musculoskeletal ultrasound of the right first dorsal compartment (to assess for tendon thickening, hypoechoic changes, peritendinous fluid, or hyperemia);  
- MRI of the right wrist/thumb (for detailed soft-tissue characterization if ultrasound is equivocal or atypical);  
- Nerve conduction study (NCS) and EMG (to rule out coexisting or alternative neuropathies, though clinically unlikely given symptom pattern);  
- Laboratory studies (ESR, CRP, CBC, TSH, RF, anti-CCP, uric acid) — appropriate for broad differential exclusion but low pretest probability for inflammatory, autoimmune, endocrine, or metabolic causes in this context.

**Next Steps (implied):**  
- Conservative management pending imaging confirmation: activity modification (avoiding thumb adduction + ulnar deviation), thumb spica splinting, NSAIDs, and consideration of corticosteroid injection into the first dorsal compartment if diagnosis confirmed.  
- Reassessment after imaging and labs to refine diagnosis and guide therapy.

No evidence supports carpal tunnel syndrome, rheumatoid arthritis, gout, infection, or cervical radiculopathy at this time.

